    <div class="section-block" id="s1">
      <h2 class="section-title">1. What is Mobile Forensics?</h2>
      <p>Mobile forensics is a branch of digital forensics focused on the recovery, preservation, extraction, and
        analysis of evidence from mobile devices such as smartphones, tablets, GPS units, and wearables. As these
        devices have become central to our daily lives, they have also become treasure troves of investigative evidence
        — capturing not just calls and messages, but locations, financial transactions, intimate conversations, and
        behavioral patterns.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>Think of a mobile phone as a digital diary that never lies. It records where you went, who you called, what
          you searched for, and sometimes even how fast your heart was beating — all in near-perfect chronological
          order.</p>
        <span class="callout-source">— Investigator's Analogy</span>
      </div>

      <p>Unlike traditional computers, mobile devices present unique forensic challenges: full-disk encryption,
        cloud-synced data, biometric locks, remote wipe capabilities, and proprietary operating systems. Yet they also
        offer unparalleled evidence richness — a single smartphone may contain more personal data than an entire filing
        cabinet of paper records.</p>

      <h3 class="sub-title">Why Mobile Forensics Matters</h3>
      <p>Today, over 6.8 billion people carry smartphones. These devices are involved in virtually every category of
        crime — from cyberbullying and financial fraud to terrorism and murder. Law enforcement agencies, corporate
        security teams, and private investigators all rely on mobile forensics to reconstruct timelines, verify alibis,
        and uncover hidden communication.</p>

      <div class="tip-box">
        <strong>Key Fact</strong>
        <p>According to cybersecurity research, mobile devices are involved in over 70% of digital crime investigations
          globally. The evidence extracted from a single phone can make or break a court case.</p>
      </div>

