      <h3 class="sub-title">Extraction Methods Explained</h3>

      <h4 class="sub-sub-title">1. Manual Extraction</h4>
      <p>The investigator physically operates the device, photographing screens or reading data visually. No special
        tools required, but extremely limited — only what's visible on screen can be captured. Useful for locked devices
        where you have a PIN, or as a supplementary method.</p>

      <h4 class="sub-sub-title">2. Logical Extraction</h4>
      <p>Uses official sync protocols: Apple's iTunes backup protocol or Android's ADB (Android Debug Bridge). Extracts
        a structured subset of data — contacts, messages, call logs, app data. Cannot recover deleted files. This is the
        most common starting point in investigations.</p>

      <h4 class="sub-sub-title">3. File System Extraction</h4>
      <p>Requires root access (Android) or jailbreak (iOS). Provides access to the entire file system structure
        including hidden directories, app sandboxes, and system files. Substantially more data than logical but risks
        damaging evidence if rooting fails.</p>

      <h4 class="sub-sub-title">4. Physical Extraction</h4>
      <p>Creates a bit-for-bit image of the device's storage memory. Because it captures raw data, it can reveal deleted
        files (whose space hasn't been overwritten) and unallocated storage. Requires specialized tools like Cellebrite
        UFED or MSAB XRY. This is the forensic gold standard.</p>

      <h4 class="sub-sub-title">5. Chip-Off Extraction</h4>
      <p>The most invasive technique: the storage chip (NAND flash) is physically removed from the device's circuit
        board and read using specialized equipment. Used only when the device is physically damaged or completely
        locked. Risks permanently destroying the device.</p>

      <div class="warning-box">
        <strong>Critical Warning</strong>
        <p>Each extraction method must be performed in a controlled forensic environment. Any modification to the
          device's state — including charging it or turning it on — can alter or destroy evidence. Always work on a
          forensic clone, never the original evidence.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 4 -->
    <div class="section-block" id="s4">
      <h2 class="section-title">4. Android Architecture &amp; Forensics</h2>
      <p>Android, developed by Google, powers roughly 72% of the world's smartphones. Its open-source nature and
        hardware diversity make it both investigator-friendly and a forensic challenge simultaneously.</p>

      <h3 class="sub-title">Android Storage Architecture</h3>

