      <h3 class="sub-title">Key Android Forensic Techniques</h3>

      <h4 class="sub-sub-title">ADB (Android Debug Bridge)</h4>
      <p>ADB is a command-line interface that allows communication between a computer and an Android device. When USB
        debugging is enabled (often required by older apps or developers), investigators can use ADB to pull backups,
        list installed packages, and in some cases access the file system without rooting.</p>
      <p class="mono">$ adb backup -apk -shared -all -f backup.ab</p>

      <h4 class="sub-sub-title">SQLite Databases — The Treasure Vault</h4>
      <p>Nearly every Android app stores its data in SQLite database files (.db). These include WhatsApp messages,
        browser history, contact details, and call logs. Even after deletion, SQLite uses a "free page" system that
        often allows recovery of recently deleted rows using forensic tools.</p>

      <h4 class="sub-sub-title">Android Keystore &amp; Encryption</h4>
      <p>Since Android 6.0, full-disk encryption is enabled by default. Since Android 7.0, file-based encryption (FBE)
        replaced full-disk encryption, encrypting files individually. This means different files may be accessible at
        different unlock states — a critical consideration for investigators.</p>

      <div class="platform-card android" style="margin:20px 0;">
        <h4><span class="dot android"></span> Critical Android Forensic Paths</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><span class="mono">/data/data/</span> — All app sandboxed data</li>
          <li><span class="mono">/data/misc/keystore/</span> — Encryption keys</li>
          <li><span class="mono">/data/system/accounts.db</span> — Google account credentials</li>
          <li><span class="mono">/sdcard/DCIM/</span> — Camera photos and videos</li>
          <li><span class="mono">/data/tombstones/</span> — App crash logs (often reveal activity)</li>
          <li><span class="mono">/proc/</span> — Running process information</li>
          <li><span class="mono">/data/data/com.whatsapp/databases/msgstore.db</span> — WhatsApp messages</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 5 -->
    <div class="section-block" id="s5">
      <h2 class="section-title">5. iOS Architecture &amp; Forensics</h2>
      <p>Apple's iOS is a closed, tightly controlled operating system used exclusively on iPhone and iPad. Its security
        model is among the strongest of any consumer device — making iOS forensics both fascinating and formidable.</p>

      <h3 class="sub-title">iOS Security Architecture</h3>
      <p>Apple's approach to security is layered through a concept called "Security Enclave" — a dedicated coprocessor
        that manages cryptographic keys. Unlike Android's more permissive model, iOS enforces strict app sandboxing,
        code signing, and hardware-backed encryption that is nearly impossible to bypass without the device passcode.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>Breaking into a locked iPhone without the passcode is like trying to open a safe while the combination is
          stored inside the safe itself. The encryption keys are derived from your passcode, so even Apple cannot
          decrypt your data without it.</p>
        <span class="callout-source">— Encryption Architecture Analogy</span>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-title">iOS Forensic Methods</h3>

      <h4 class="sub-sub-title">iTunes/Finder Backup</h4>
      <p>The most accessible method — if the device is unlocked and trusted. Creates an encrypted or unencrypted backup
        containing contacts, messages, photos, app data, and more. Encrypted backups contain more data (health, keychain
        passwords) but require the backup password.</p>

      <h4 class="sub-sub-title">iCloud Forensics</h4>
      <p>With legal authority and Apple's cooperation, investigators can request iCloud data. This may include iCloud
        Drive, Photos, iMessages (if backed up), location history, and app data. iCloud data is stored in Apple's
        servers and may persist even after a factory reset.</p>

      <h4 class="sub-sub-title">Advanced Logical (GrayKey / Cellebrite Premium)</h4>
      <p>Tools like GrayKey exploit software vulnerabilities to bypass passcode attempts or extract more data than
        standard logical methods allow. GrayKey can perform brute-force attacks on the passcode by exploiting low-level
        bootloader vulnerabilities. Access to these tools is restricted to law enforcement.</p>

      <h4 class="sub-sub-title">Checkm8 Exploit</h4>
      <p>Discovered in 2019, Checkm8 is a permanent bootrom vulnerability affecting iPhones from 5s to X (A5–A11 chips).
        It cannot be patched via software updates, giving forensic investigators a persistent low-level access point for
        affected devices.</p>

      <div class="platform-card ios" style="margin:20px 0;">
        <h4><span class="dot ios"></span> Critical iOS Forensic Locations</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><span class="mono">/var/mobile/Library/SMS/</span> — SMS and iMessage databases</li>
          <li><span class="mono">/var/mobile/Library/CallHistoryDB/</span> — Call logs</li>
          <li><span class="mono">/var/mobile/Media/DCIM/</span> — Photos and videos</li>
          <li><span class="mono">/var/mobile/Library/Safari/</span> — Browser history, bookmarks</li>
          <li><span class="mono">/var/mobile/Library/Accounts/</span> — Account credentials</li>
          <li><span class="mono">/var/Keychains/</span> — Stored passwords and certificates</li>
          <li><span class="mono">/var/mobile/Library/Caches/</span> — App caches (often contain deleted data)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

