      <h2 class="section-title">6. Android vs iOS — Key Differences</h2>
      <p>Understanding how these platforms differ forensically is essential for choosing the right approach in an
        investigation.</p>

      <table class="compare-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Attribute</th>
            <th><span class="tag-android">Android</span></th>
            <th><span class="tag-ios">iOS</span></th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Openness</td>
            <td>Open-source (AOSP); highly fragmented across manufacturers</td>
            <td>Closed, proprietary; tightly controlled by Apple</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>File System</td>
            <td>ext4 / f2fs; familiar to Linux forensics experts</td>
            <td>APFS (Apple File System); copy-on-write, encrypted by default</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Encryption</td>
            <td>FBE (Android 10+); varies by manufacturer and version</td>
            <td>Hardware AES-256 tied to Secure Enclave; passcode-derived keys</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Root / Jailbreak</td>
            <td>Root access more common; many methods available</td>
            <td>Jailbreaking is harder; version-specific exploits required</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
