    <!-- SECTION 7 -->
    <div class="section-block" id="s7">
      <h2 class="section-title">7. The Forensic Investigation Process</h2>
      <p>Mobile forensics follows a methodical, court-admissible process. Every step must be documented to ensure
        evidence integrity.</p>

      <ul class="step-list">
        <li>
          <span class="step-num">01</span>
          <div class="step-content">
            <strong>Seizure &amp; Scene Documentation</strong>
            <span>Photograph the device in its original state. Note battery level, network connectivity, visible
              notifications. Place device in airplane mode or a Faraday bag immediately to prevent remote wipe and
              preserve cellular evidence.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-num">02</span>
          <div class="step-content">
            <strong>Identification &amp; Documentation</strong>
            <span>Record device make, model, IMEI, serial number, iOS/Android version, and storage capacity. Photograph
              front and back of the device. Complete chain of custody form.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-num">03</span>
          <div class="step-content">
            <strong>Preservation</strong>
            <span>Clone the device storage to a forensic image before any extraction. Keep the original device powered
              (if already on) to maintain volatile data like RAM. Use write blockers where applicable. Calculate
              MD5/SHA-256 hash of extracted images.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-num">04</span>
          <div class="step-content">
            <strong>Extraction</strong>
            <span>Choose the appropriate extraction method based on device type, OS version, lock state, and legal
              authority. Document every tool, command, and outcome. Attempt least-invasive method first.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-num">05</span>
          <div class="step-content">
            <strong>Analysis</strong>
            <span>Parse extracted data using forensic analysis tools. Reconstruct timelines. Cross-reference
              communication records with location data. Search for keywords, recover deleted files, analyze
              metadata.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-num">06</span>
          <div class="step-content">
            <strong>Reporting</strong>
            <span>Prepare a detailed forensic report documenting methodology, tools used, hash verification, findings,
              and conclusions. Report must be reproducible and withstand cross-examination in court.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="tip-box">
        <strong>Faraday Bag Tip</strong>
        <p>A Faraday bag is a metal-shielded enclosure that blocks all RF signals (cellular, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, GPS).
          Always place a seized device in a Faraday bag if it cannot be immediately examined. This prevents remote wipe
          commands and preserves the device's last network state as evidence.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 8 -->
    <div class="section-block" id="s8">
      <h2 class="section-title">8. Common Artifacts &amp; Evidence Types</h2>
      <p>Mobile devices generate an enormous variety of forensic artifacts. Here are the most investigatively
        significant categories:</p>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="platform-card">
          <h4 style="font-size:15px;">📞 Communication Artifacts</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>SMS/MMS messages with timestamps</li>
            <li>iMessage conversations (end-to-end encrypted but locally stored)</li>
            <li>WhatsApp, Telegram, Signal chat databases</li>
            <li>Email headers and bodies (cached)</li>
            <li>VoIP call logs (WhatsApp, FaceTime, Skype)</li>
            <li>Contact lists and deleted contacts</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="platform-card">
          <h4 style="font-size:15px;">📍 Location Artifacts</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>GPS coordinates from photos (EXIF metadata)</li>
            <li>Cell tower triangulation logs</li>
            <li>Wi-Fi network connection history</li>
            <li>Google Timeline / Apple Significant Locations</li>
            <li>Maps search history and directions</li>
            <li>Uber/Lyft ride records (app databases)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="platform-card">
          <h4 style="font-size:15px;">🌐 Internet Artifacts</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Browser history (Chrome, Safari databases)</li>
            <li>Cached web pages and images</li>
            <li>Downloaded files with timestamps</li>
            <li>Cookies and session tokens</li>
            <li>Search engine queries</li>
            <li>Private/incognito mode remnants (often still cached)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="platform-card">
          <h4 style="font-size:15px;">💼 Device &amp; System Artifacts</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Power-on/off timestamps</li>
            <li>App installation and deletion history</li>
            <li>Bluetooth device pairing history</li>
            <li>System logs and crash reports</li>
            <li>Screen time and app usage statistics</li>
            <li>Notification history (recent lockscreen events)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-title">EXIF Metadata — Hidden in Plain Sight</h3>
      <p>Every photo taken on a smartphone embeds EXIF (Exchangeable Image File Format) metadata. This data can include
        GPS coordinates, timestamp, device model, even lens aperture settings. In investigations, a single photo posted
        to social media — before platforms strip metadata — can pinpoint a suspect's exact location at a precise moment
        in time.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>A 2012 bank robbery case was cracked after the suspect posted a selfie online from inside the bank. The EXIF
          metadata embedded the exact GPS coordinates and timestamp — matching perfectly with the crime scene location
          and time of robbery.</p>
        <span class="callout-source">— Real-World EXIF Evidence Example</span>
      </div>
    </div>

