    <!-- SECTION 10 -->
    <div class="section-block" id="s10">
      <h2 class="section-title">10. Legal &amp; Ethical Considerations</h2>
      <p>Mobile forensics sits at the intersection of technology, law, and civil liberties. Improperly obtained evidence
        can be inadmissible in court — and in many jurisdictions, unauthorized device access is a criminal offense.</p>

      <h3 class="sub-title">Legal Framework</h3>
      <p>In most jurisdictions, accessing a mobile device's data without authorization requires either the owner's
        consent or a valid court order (search warrant). The Fourth Amendment in the US, Article 8 of the European
        Convention on Human Rights, and equivalent laws globally protect citizens from unreasonable searches.</p>

      <h4 class="sub-sub-title">Riley v. California (2014)</h4>
      <p>The US Supreme Court unanimously ruled that police need a warrant to search the contents of a smartphone during
        an arrest. This landmark decision established that mobile phones contain a qualitatively different type of data
        than a wallet or purse — the entire record of a person's private life.</p>

      <div class="warning-box">
        <strong>Legal Warning</strong>
        <p>Unauthorized access to a mobile device — including that of an employee by an employer, or a spouse's device —
          may constitute a criminal offense under the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (US), Computer Misuse Act (UK), or
          Information Technology Act (India, Section 66). Always obtain proper legal authority before any examination.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-title">Chain of Custody</h3>
      <p>Every piece of evidence must have a documented and unbroken chain of custody — a chronological record of who
        has had possession of the evidence, when, and for what purpose. Any gap in this chain can render evidence
        inadmissible. Digital forensics reports must document hash values to prove data was not modified.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 11 -->
    <div class="section-block" id="s11">
      <h2 class="section-title">11. Anti-Forensics &amp; Challenges</h2>
      <p>Suspects increasingly use anti-forensic techniques to obscure, destroy, or complicate the recovery of digital
        evidence. Mobile forensics investigators must be familiar with these challenges.</p>

      <h3 class="sub-title">Common Anti-Forensic Techniques</h3>

      <ul class="step-list">
        <li>
          <span class="step-num" style="background:#c0392b;">!</span>
          <div class="step-content">
            <strong>Remote Wipe</strong>
            <span>Using "Find My iPhone" or "Find My Device", suspects can wipe a phone remotely the moment it connects
              to any network. Faraday isolation is the primary countermeasure.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-num" style="background:#c0392b;">!</span>
          <div class="step-content">
            <strong>Encrypted Messaging Apps</strong>
            <span>Signal, Wickr, and Telegram "Secret Chats" use end-to-end encryption with optional message
              auto-deletion. Once deleted, messages may be cryptographically unrecoverable even with physical
              access.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-num" style="background:#c0392b;">!</span>
          <div class="step-content">
            <strong>Strong Passcodes / Biometric Lock</strong>
            <span>A strong alphanumeric passcode makes brute-force impractical. Even GrayKey reportedly takes days to
              years for complex passcodes. With iOS, 10 failed attempts may trigger data erasure.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-num" style="background:#c0392b;">!</span>
          <div class="step-content">
            <strong>Burner Phones &amp; SIM Swapping</strong>
            <span>Criminals may use multiple cheap prepaid phones ("burners") that are discarded after use, making it
              impossible to recover evidence from a device that was never retained.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-num" style="background:#c0392b;">!</span>
          <div class="step-content">
            <strong>Factory Reset</strong>
            <span>A factory reset erases user data partitions. While forensic tools can sometimes recover data from
              unwritten flash memory blocks, a reset followed by filling storage with new data makes recovery extremely
              difficult.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 12 -->
    <div class="section-block" id="s12">
      <h2 class="section-title">12. Advantages &amp; Disadvantages</h2>

      <div class="pros-cons">
        <div class="pros-box">
          <h5>Advantages of Mobile Forensics</h5>
          <ul>
            <li>Rich evidence density — phones contain location, communication, and behavioral data</li>
            <li>Timestamped records provide precise timelines</li>
            <li>Cloud backups provide redundant evidence even if device is destroyed</li>
            <li>EXIF metadata can place suspects at crime scenes</li>
            <li>Deleted data is often recoverable from flash memory</li>
            <li>App databases provide granular behavioral insight</li>
            <li>Corroborates or refutes witness testimony objectively</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons-box">
          <h5>Disadvantages &amp; Challenges</h5>
          <ul>
            <li>Encryption makes data inaccessible without passcode</li>
            <li>Rapid OS updates can invalidate forensic tools</li>
            <li>Legal barriers — warrants required, cross-jurisdictional complexity</li>
            <li>Anti-forensic techniques can destroy evidence permanently</li>
            <li>High cost of commercial forensic tools</li>
            <li>Cloud data requires separate legal process to obtain</li>
            <li>Data fragmentation across multiple devices and platforms</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 13 -->
    <div class="section-block" id="s13">
      <h2 class="section-title">13. Real-World Case Studies</h2>

      <div class="case-study">
        <div class="cs-label">Case Study 01 — United States</div>
        <h4>San Bernardino Terrorism Case (2016)</h4>
        <p>After the 2015 San Bernardino attack, the FBI obtained the shooter's locked iPhone 5C. Unable to bypass iOS
          encryption, the FBI sought a court order compelling Apple to create a backdoor. Apple refused on privacy
          grounds. The FBI ultimately paid a third-party firm (reportedly Israeli firm Cellebrite or NSO Group)
          approximately $1 million to unlock the device using an undisclosed exploit. The case transformed mobile
          forensics legislation globally and accelerated development of advanced bypass tools.</p>
        <p><strong>Forensic Lesson:</strong> Even the world's most powerful law enforcement agencies face real barriers
          with modern mobile encryption. Legal frameworks and technical capabilities must evolve together.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="case-study">
        <div class="cs-label">Case Study 02 — India</div>
        <h4>Sheena Bora Murder Case (2015)</h4>
        <p>In this high-profile Indian murder case, mobile forensics played a pivotal role. Investigators analyzed call
          detail records (CDRs) from multiple phones to establish when and where the suspect and victim communicated,
          and to refute alibis. WhatsApp message timestamps and deleted messages recovered from the accused's phone were
          presented as evidence, helping reconstruct the timeline of events leading to the murder.</p>
        <p><strong>Forensic Lesson:</strong> Call records, even without message content, can construct powerful
          timelines. Deleted messages on Android devices can often be recovered from unallocated SQLite space.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="case-study">
        <div class="cs-label">Case Study 03 — Corporate Investigation</div>
        <h4>Trade Secret Theft via Personal iPhone</h4>
        <p>A senior engineer at a semiconductor company was suspected of exfiltrating proprietary chip designs to a
          competitor. Corporate forensic investigators, working under proper legal authority, performed an iCloud
          forensic acquisition of the engineer's company account. Analysis of iCloud Drive upload logs, AirDrop history,
          and email attachments revealed that terabytes of design files had been transferred to personal storage the
          week before the engineer resigned. The iCloud metadata timestamps proved deliberate, planned exfiltration.</p>
        <p><strong>Forensic Lesson:</strong> Cloud forensics can sometimes yield evidence even when the physical device
          is unavailable or wiped.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="case-study">
        <div class="cs-label">Case Study 04 — Drug Trafficking</div>
        <h4>Operation Trojan Shield / ANOM (2021)</h4>
        <p>The FBI secretly ran an encrypted phone company called ANOM, used by thousands of organized crime members
          globally. When the operation concluded, forensic investigators had access to 27 million messages. Mobile
          forensic analysis of ANOM devices — running a customized Android OS — revealed drug shipment routes,
          assassination plots, and money laundering operations. Over 800 arrests were made across 16 countries. This
          case demonstrated the forensic power of combining mobile device analysis with intelligence operations.</p>
        <p><strong>Forensic Lesson:</strong> Purpose-built forensic backdoors can yield unprecedented evidence, but
          raise significant civil liberty questions.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

